1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:02,931
This is a human hand.

2
00:00:03,131 --> 00:00:05,294
About ten centimeters across.

3
00:00:06,194 --> 00:00:09,801
Now let's zoom out, ten times wider at
every step,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,926
until we reach the edge of everything we
can see.

5
00:00:13,426 --> 00:00:16,780
A person, lying in the grass on a summer
afternoon.

6
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:20,023
A blanket, a few trees, and a footpath.

7
00:00:20,523 --> 00:00:22,348
This is the Sheep Meadow,

8
00:00:22,548 --> 00:00:24,479
in New York's Central Park.

9
00:00:24,979 --> 00:00:27,291
The park is four kilometers long,

10
00:00:27,491 --> 00:00:30,239
a green rectangle cut out of the city
grid.

11
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:34,172
Manhattan: an island twenty-one kilometers
long,

12
00:00:34,372 --> 00:00:37,204
home to more than one and a half million
people.

13
00:00:37,704 --> 00:00:38,999
The whole city,

14
00:00:39,199 --> 00:00:42,877
wrapped around one of the largest natural
harbors in the world.

15
00:00:43,377 --> 00:00:45,600
The east coast of North America,

16
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,220
and the curve of the planet starts to
show.

17
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:50,806
The Earth.

18
00:00:51,006 --> 00:00:54,268
Twelve thousand seven hundred kilometers
across.

19
00:00:55,968 --> 00:01:00,570
The International Space Station orbits
just four hundred kilometers up,

20
00:01:00,770 --> 00:01:03,136
barely above the surface at this scale.

21
00:01:03,636 --> 00:01:04,709
The Moon,

22
00:01:04,909 --> 00:01:08,214
three hundred eighty-four thousand
kilometers away.

23
00:01:08,414 --> 00:01:11,134
Light makes the trip in just over a
second.

24
00:01:11,634 --> 00:01:15,631
Only a handful of humans have ever
traveled this far from home.

25
00:01:16,131 --> 00:01:17,204
The Sun.

26
00:01:17,404 --> 00:01:20,420
A hundred and fifty million kilometers
away.

27
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:23,379
Its light takes about eight minutes to
reach us.

28
00:01:23,879 --> 00:01:26,363
Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars:

29
00:01:26,563 --> 00:01:28,310
the rocky inner planets,

30
00:01:28,510 --> 00:01:30,989
and past them, the asteroid belt.

31
00:01:31,489 --> 00:01:32,945
Then the giants:

32
00:01:33,145 --> 00:01:35,394
Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus,

33
00:01:35,594 --> 00:01:38,933
and Neptune, four and a half billion
kilometers out.

34
00:01:39,433 --> 00:01:42,948
Voyager 1, launched in nineteen
seventy-seven,

35
00:01:43,148 --> 00:01:46,107
is the farthest object humans have ever
built.

36
00:01:46,307 --> 00:01:48,510
It's nearly a full light-day away.

37
00:01:49,010 --> 00:01:52,238
A light-year is nearly ten trillion
kilometers.

38
00:01:52,438 --> 00:01:56,776
Around the Sun, the Oort cloud may hold
trillions of icy comets,

39
00:01:56,976 --> 00:01:59,315
stretching more than a light-year out.

40
00:01:59,815 --> 00:02:01,541
Four light-years away:

41
00:02:01,741 --> 00:02:04,221
Proxima Centauri, the nearest star.

42
00:02:04,421 --> 00:02:08,276
Voyager would need more than seventy
thousand years to get there.

43
00:02:08,776 --> 00:02:10,545
Within ten light-years,

44
00:02:10,745 --> 00:02:13,038
there are only about a dozen stars.

45
00:02:13,538 --> 00:02:16,057
Nearly every star you can see at night

46
00:02:16,257 --> 00:02:18,798
lies within a few thousand light-years.

47
00:02:20,098 --> 00:02:21,409
The Milky Way.

48
00:02:21,609 --> 00:02:24,107
A hundred thousand light-years across,

49
00:02:24,307 --> 00:02:26,647
with hundreds of billions of stars.

50
00:02:28,647 --> 00:02:33,573
We're out here, in a quiet arm, twenty-six
thousand light-years from the center.

51
00:02:33,773 --> 00:02:38,637
There, a black hole four million times
heavier than the Sun sits waiting.

52
00:02:39,137 --> 00:02:43,605
The Sun takes about two hundred thirty
million years to go around once.

53
00:02:43,805 --> 00:02:45,576
The last time we were here,

54
00:02:45,776 --> 00:02:48,279
the first dinosaurs were just appearing.

55
00:02:48,779 --> 00:02:50,211
The Local Group:

56
00:02:50,411 --> 00:02:52,536
the Milky Way, Andromeda,

57
00:02:52,736 --> 00:02:55,007
and dozens of smaller galaxies.

58
00:02:55,507 --> 00:02:58,883
Andromeda is two and a half million
light-years away.

59
00:02:59,083 --> 00:03:02,237
On a dark night you can see it with your
naked eye,

60
00:03:02,437 --> 00:03:06,090
as light that set off long before our
species existed.

61
00:03:06,590 --> 00:03:08,639
Galaxies gather in groups,

62
00:03:08,839 --> 00:03:10,565
groups into clusters,

63
00:03:10,765 --> 00:03:13,138
and clusters into superclusters.

64
00:03:13,638 --> 00:03:17,253
Ours is called Laniakea, Hawaiian for
immense heaven:

65
00:03:17,453 --> 00:03:19,844
about a hundred thousand galaxies,

66
00:03:20,044 --> 00:03:22,864
spread across five hundred million
light-years.

67
00:03:23,364 --> 00:03:24,741
Zoom out again,

68
00:03:24,941 --> 00:03:28,213
and the galaxies line up into filaments
and walls,

69
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:30,570
around enormous empty voids.

70
00:03:30,770 --> 00:03:32,191
The cosmic web.

71
00:03:33,691 --> 00:03:36,093
Every point of light here is a galaxy.

72
00:03:36,593 --> 00:03:38,430
The observable universe.

73
00:03:38,630 --> 00:03:41,205
Ninety-three billion light-years across,

74
00:03:41,405 --> 00:03:44,225
holding hundreds of billions of galaxies.

75
00:03:46,725 --> 00:03:50,091
The universe is thirteen point eight
billion years old,

76
00:03:50,291 --> 00:03:52,936
but space has been stretching the whole
time,

77
00:03:53,136 --> 00:03:58,092
so the farthest light we see came from
places that are now much farther away.

78
00:03:58,592 --> 00:04:01,229
At the edge is the oldest light there is:

79
00:04:01,429 --> 00:04:03,505
the afterglow of the Big Bang.

80
00:04:04,005 --> 00:04:07,194
Beyond it, light hasn't had time to reach
us yet.

81
00:04:07,394 --> 00:04:10,774
Nobody knows how far the universe really
goes.

82
00:04:11,274 --> 00:04:15,140
And since light reaches us from every
direction at the same speed,

83
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:18,070
the center of the observable universe

84
00:04:18,270 --> 00:04:19,858
is right where you are.

85
00:04:20,958 --> 00:04:22,461
So let's go back.

86
00:04:29,461 --> 00:04:30,870
A human hand.

87
00:04:31,070 --> 00:04:33,124
About twenty-eight steps of ten

88
00:04:33,324 --> 00:04:35,720
from the edge of everything we can see.

89
00:04:36,220 --> 00:04:37,622
Let's zoom out.
